{
  "id": "py-13-nested-if",
  "track": "python",
  "unit": "py-u2",
  "order": 13,
  "title": "Nested decisions and the one-line if",
  "summary": "Put an if inside another if, flatten it when you can, and pick a value with a one-line if.",
  "language": "python",
  "minutes": 20,
  "visual": "memory",
  "visuals": [
    "branch",
    "expr",
    "memory"
  ],
  "intro": {
    "text": "An if can sit inside another if's block. The inner if is only reached when the outer test is True. Each level moves 4 more spaces to the right:\n\n```\nif logged_in:\n    if is_admin:\n        print(\"Admin panel\")\n```\n\nDeep nesting gets hard to read. Often you can flatten it with `and` or with elif.\n\nWhen you only need to **choose a value**, there is a one-line if:\n\n```\nlabel = \"even\" if n % 2 == 0 else \"odd\"\n```\n\nRead it as: label is \"even\" if the test is True, otherwise \"odd\". Only the chosen side is worked out. Use it for short choices; use a full if for anything longer.",
    "analogy": "Nested ifs are like doors inside a building. You can only reach the inner door after you are through the front door. The one-line if is like a light switch: one quick choice between two values."
  },
